<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<Patch>

  <!--
    Marks the game's ceremonial garments with FTO_Ceremonial, which is what the no-building path
    looks for.

    Patched by CRITERION, not by a list of defNames: every tag below is a real vanilla apparel
    tag, so any modded garment already carrying one of them joins in with no patch of its own.
    A census of every Apparel_*.xml in Core and the DLCs gives, for garments meant to be worn as
    display rather than as protection:

      Royal (9)            the royal wardrobe, plus Core's cape
      RoyalRobe (1)        the royal robe
      Robe (1)             the plain robe
      Cape (1)             Core's cape again, which carries both tags
      BestowerHood (1)     the bestowing ceremony's own hood
      HoraxianCeremonial (1)   Anomaly's ceremonial garb - the name says it

    Twelve defs in all, the overlaps removed. THREE OF THEM ARE ABSTRACT BASES -
    ApparelShirtRoyalTier2Base, ApparelVestRoyalTier5Base and ApparelHatRoyalTier2 - and that is
    not an accident to be tidied away: tagging a base is how the ruffled shirt, the corset, the
    royal vest, the ladies' hat and the top hat inherit the mark without being named. Nine concrete
    defs carry the tag themselves and five more inherit it, so fourteen vanilla garments end up
    ceremonial. Counting the tag rather than the garment is the reason the two figures differ.

    Mod authors who want a garment to count only need to add <li>FTO_Ceremonial</li> to its
    apparel tags. Nothing else in the mod reads a defName.

    No second operation is needed here, unlike the outfit stand patch: selecting on
    apparel/tags/li guarantees the tags node exists, and this operation cannot make its own
    predicate true a second time.
  -->

  <Operation Class="PatchOperationConditional">
    <xpath>/Defs/ThingDef[apparel/tags/li="Royal" or apparel/tags/li="RoyalRobe" or apparel/tags/li="Robe" or apparel/tags/li="Cape" or apparel/tags/li="BestowerHood" or apparel/tags/li="HoraxianCeremonial"]/apparel/tags</xpath>
    <match Class="PatchOperationAdd">
      <xpath>/Defs/ThingDef[apparel/tags/li="Royal" or apparel/tags/li="RoyalRobe" or apparel/tags/li="Robe" or apparel/tags/li="Cape" or apparel/tags/li="BestowerHood" or apparel/tags/li="HoraxianCeremonial"]/apparel/tags</xpath>
      <value>
        <li>FTO_Ceremonial</li>
      </value>
    </match>
  </Operation>

</Patch>
